    "content": "某經濟體只生產食物與衣服兩種產品。\n(a) 說明生產可能性界線上的點與界線以內的點，分別代表甚麼。\n(b) 為何生產可能性界線一般向外凸（凹向原點）？\n(c) 舉出兩項會令整條界線向外移的因素。",
    "explanation": "(b) 的正確理由是機會成本遞增：生產要素並非同質，各自適合生產不同產品。當一種產品的產量持續增加時，須動用愈來愈不適合生產該產品的要素，故每多生產一單位所須放棄的另一產品數量遞增，界線因而向外凸。只寫「因為資源有限」並未解釋形狀 —— 資源有限只解釋了界線的存在，並不解釋它為何不是直線。\n(c) 須分辨「界線向外移」與「界線上移動」。前者要求生產能力本身提升（要素數量增加或技術進步），後者只是產品組合的轉換。答「多生產食物」是界線上的移動，不得分。",
    "referenceAnswer": "(a) 界線上的點：代表在現有資源與技術之下，充分及有效率地運用所有生產要素所能達致的產量組合。此時不可能在不減少一種產品的產量的情況下增加另一種產品的產量。\n界線以內的點：代表資源未被充分運用或運用效率偏低（例如存在失業或閒置設備）。此時可以同時增加兩種產品的產量，並不需要作出取捨。\n\n(b) 因為機會成本遞增。\n生產要素並非同質：某些要素較適合生產食物，另一些較適合生產衣服。當衣服的產量持續增加時，必須把愈來愈不適合生產衣服的要素（原本較適合生產食物者）轉用於衣服。故每多生產一單位衣服所須放棄的食物數量逐漸增加，界線的斜率愈來愈陡，形狀因而向外凸。\n（若各項生產要素完全同質，機會成本固定，界線便會是一條直線。）\n\n(c) 任舉兩項：\n1. 生產要素數量增加，例如人口增長令勞動力增加，或發現新的自然資源。\n2. 技術進步，令同樣數量的生產要素能生產更多產品。\n3. 教育與培訓改善人力資本的質素，提升勞動生產力。\n4. 資本累積，例如新增機器與廠房。\n須注意：純粹改變兩種產品的產量組合屬於界線【上】的移動，並不會令界線本身外移。",
    "marks": 1,
    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) A point on the frontier: an output combination attainable when all factors of production are fully and efficiently employed, given existing resources and technology. At such a point one product cannot be increased without reducing the other.\nA point inside the frontier: resources are not fully employed or are used inefficiently (for example unemployment or idle plant). Here both products can be increased at once, with no trade-off required.\n\n(b) Because opportunity cost increases.\nFactors of production are not homogeneous: some are better suited to food, others to clothing. As clothing output keeps rising, factors progressively less suited to clothing (those better suited to food) must be switched to it. Each extra unit of clothing therefore costs more food forgone, the frontier becomes steeper, and its shape is concave to the origin.\n(If all factors were perfectly homogeneous, opportunity cost would be constant and the frontier a straight line.)\n\n(c) Any two of:\n1. An increase in the quantity of factors, for example population growth raising the labour force, or the discovery of new natural resources.\n2. Technological progress, letting the same quantity of factors produce more output.\n3. Education and training improving the quality of human capital and raising labour productivity.\n4. Capital accumulation, for example additional machinery and factories.\nNote that merely changing the mix of the two products is a movement ALONG the frontier and does not shift the frontier itself.",
    "contentEn": "An economy produces only food and clothing.\n(a) State what a point on the production possibility frontier and a point inside it each represent.\n(b) Why is the production possibility frontier usually concave to the origin?\n(c) State two factors that would shift the entire frontier outwards.",
    "explanationEn": "The correct reason in (b) is increasing opportunity cost: factors of production are not homogeneous and are suited to different products. As output of one product keeps rising, factors progressively less suited to it must be drawn in, so each extra unit costs more of the other product forgone, making the frontier concave to the origin. Writing only \"because resources are limited\" does not explain the shape — scarcity explains why a frontier exists, not why it is not a straight line.\nPart (c) requires separating a shift of the frontier from a movement along it. A shift requires productive capacity itself to rise (more factors or better technology); a movement along it is only a change in the product mix. \"Produce more food\" is a movement along the frontier and earns nothing."
