    "difficulty": "medium",
    "year": 0,
    "content": "甲國生產一單位布需 4 小時，生產一單位酒需 8 小時；乙國生產一單位布需 6 小時，生產一單位酒需 6 小時。\n(a) 哪一國在生產布方面具有絕對優勢？哪一國在生產酒方面具有比較優勢？\n(b) 說明兩國應如何分工，並解釋分工為何能令雙方得益。",
    "explanation": "本題的分辨點在於絕對優勢與比較優勢是兩個不同的準則。甲國生產兩種產品所需的時間都較少，故在兩者皆具絕對優勢；但比較優勢須看【機會成本】而非所需時間。\n甲國生產一單位酒的機會成本為 2 單位布（8 ÷ 4）；乙國為 1 單位布（6 ÷ 6）。乙國的機會成本較低，故在酒方面具比較優勢。\n最常見的錯誤是見甲國「樣樣都快」便斷定甲國應該自行生產兩種產品、貿易對其無益。此結論忽略了甲國把時間集中於機會成本較低的產品（布）所能取得的額外產量 —— 得益的來源是機會成本的差異，而非絕對效率的高低。",
    "referenceAnswer": "(a) 絕對優勢（布）：甲國。甲國生產一單位布只需 4 小時，少於乙國的 6 小時。\n（事實上甲國生產酒亦只需 8 小時，少於乙國的 6 小時以外的比較須另計 —— 就酒而言乙國需 6 小時，較甲國的 8 小時為少，故乙國在酒方面具絕對優勢。）\n\n比較優勢（酒）：乙國。\n計算機會成本：\n甲國：生產 1 單位酒需 8 小時，同樣的 8 小時可生產 2 單位布，故機會成本為 2 單位布。\n乙國：生產 1 單位酒需 6 小時，同樣的 6 小時可生產 1 單位布，故機會成本為 1 單位布。\n乙國生產酒的機會成本較低，故在酒方面具比較優勢。\n\n(b) 分工方式：甲國專門生產布，乙國專門生產酒，然後互相貿易。\n\n得益的原因：\n甲國生產布的機會成本為 0.5 單位酒（4 ÷ 8），乙國為 1 單位酒（6 ÷ 6），故甲國在布方面具比較優勢；乙國在酒方面具比較優勢（如 (a) 所示）。\n各國專門生產本身機會成本較低的產品，可令兩國合計的總產量上升。以同樣的總工時計算，專門化之後所生產的布與酒的總量，均多於兩國各自自給自足時的總量。\n總產量增加後，透過貿易按雙方接受的交換比率分配，兩國所能消費的產品組合皆可超越其本身的生產可能性界線，故雙方均可得益。\n須注意得益的來源是【機會成本的差異】，而非絕對效率的高低；即使一國在所有產品上皆具絕對優勢，只要兩國的機會成本不同，分工仍然對雙方有利。",
    "marks": 1,
    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) Absolute advantage in cloth: Country A. It needs only 4 hours per unit against Country B’s 6.\n(For wine, Country B needs 6 hours against Country A’s 8, so Country B has the absolute advantage in wine.)\n\nComparative advantage in wine: Country B.\nOpportunity costs:\nCountry A: one unit of wine takes 8 hours, and the same 8 hours would make 2 units of cloth, so the opportunity cost is 2 units of cloth.\nCountry B: one unit of wine takes 6 hours, and the same 6 hours would make 1 unit of cloth, so the opportunity cost is 1 unit of cloth.\nCountry B has the lower opportunity cost for wine and therefore the comparative advantage.\n\n(b) Specialisation: Country A should specialise in cloth and Country B in wine, and the two should trade.\n\nWhy both gain:\nCountry A’s opportunity cost of cloth is 0.5 units of wine (4 ÷ 8) against Country B’s 1 unit (6 ÷ 6), so Country A has the comparative advantage in cloth, and Country B in wine as shown in (a).\nWhen each country specialises in the good for which its own opportunity cost is lower, combined total output rises. For the same total hours worked, specialisation yields more cloth and more wine in total than the two countries produce under self-sufficiency.\nWith total output higher, trade at any mutually acceptable rate of exchange lets both countries consume combinations beyond their own production possibility frontiers, so both gain.\nNote that the gains come from DIFFERENCES IN OPPORTUNITY COST, not from absolute efficiency: even if one country has an absolute advantage in everything, specialisation still benefits both provided their opportunity costs differ.",
    "contentEn": "Country A needs 4 hours to produce one unit of cloth and 8 hours for one unit of wine; Country B needs 6 hours for cloth and 6 hours for wine.\n(a) Which country has an absolute advantage in cloth? Which has a comparative advantage in wine?\n(b) State how the two countries should specialise, and explain why specialisation makes both better off.",
    "explanationEn": "The discriminator is that absolute and comparative advantage are different criteria. Country A takes less time for both products and so has an absolute advantage in both; comparative advantage, however, depends on OPPORTUNITY COST rather than hours required.\nFor Country A the opportunity cost of one unit of wine is 2 units of cloth (8 ÷ 4); for Country B it is 1 unit of cloth (6 ÷ 6). Country B has the lower opportunity cost and therefore the comparative advantage in wine.\nThe commonest error is to see that Country A is faster at everything and conclude that it should make both goods itself and gains nothing from trade. That ignores the extra output Country A obtains by concentrating its hours on the good with the lower opportunity cost (cloth) — the gains come from differences in opportunity cost, not from differences in absolute efficiency."
